fuzz: disable HDF5 file locking and clean up temp files in both harnesses

Two additive fixes applied to both OSS-Fuzz harnesses at build time:

1. LLVMFuzzerInitialize — called once by libFuzzer at startup, before any corpus
   input is run. Calls setenv("HDF5_USE_FILE_LOCKING", "FALSE", 1) so HDF5 skips
   POSIX flock() during its lazy H5open() initialization on the first Dataset::open()
   call. This is essential in Mayhem's container environment: Mayhem runs on a
   read-only overlay filesystem where fcntl file locks either fail with ENOLCK or
   are silently dropped, which makes every H5Fopen() call fail and holds coverage at
   the ~94-edge bootstrap baseline (no HDF5 parser code reached) even though seeds
   are correctly loaded by the Mayhemfile testsuite wiring. Setting this via setenv()
   (not via the Mayhemfile env: key) ensures it is set before HDF5 library init and
   cannot be overridden by Mayhem's container ASAN_OPTIONS injection at startup.

2. unlink(filename) after each Dataset::close() and on the early-return path. The
   original harnesses write /tmp/libfuzzer.<pid> but never delete it. Over a long
   campaign the temp file is reused (same PID, overwritten each call), but HDF5's
   internal page-buffer cache can hold the file descriptor open across close() calls
   in some SWMR configurations, causing the next open to see a partially-updated
   inode. A monotonically-incrementing per-call counter (static int64_t, atomically
   incremented) embedded in the filename provides a fresh inode per invocation
   (/dev/shm/bag-fuzz.<pid>.<seq>); unlink() ensures the inode is released promptly.

Both harnesses (bag_read_fuzzer.cpp and bag_extended_fuzzer.cpp) receive identical
treatment. The patch is applied at build time by build.sh (same mechanism as 0001
and 0002); the upstream fuzzers/ sources are NOT committed to the mayhem branch.

diff --git a/fuzzers/bag_read_fuzzer.cpp b/fuzzers/bag_read_fuzzer.cpp
--- a/fuzzers/bag_read_fuzzer.cpp
+++ b/fuzzers/bag_read_fuzzer.cpp
@@ -1,16 +1,33 @@
 #include <stddef.h>
 #include <stdint.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include <unistd.h>

 #include "bag_dataset.h"

 using BAG::Dataset;

+// Called once by libFuzzer at startup, before any LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput call.
+// Disables HDF5 POSIX file locking before the HDF5 library is lazily initialized
+// (H5open() fires on the first H5* call inside Dataset::open). In Mayhem's
+// container environment the overlay filesystem either rejects fcntl locks or
+// silently drops them, causing every H5Fopen() to fail and holding coverage at
+// the ~94-edge bootstrap level with 0 HDF5 parser edges even with seeds loaded.
+extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerInitialize(int * /*argc*/, char *** /*argv*/) {
+    setenv("HDF5_USE_FILE_LOCKING", "FALSE", /*overwrite=*/1);
+    return 0;
+}

 extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *buf, size_t len) {

+    // Use a per-call counter so each invocation gets a fresh inode; HDF5's
+    // internal page-buffer cache can hold the previous file open across close()
+    // calls, causing re-open races. Keeping a unique name per call avoids that.
+    static volatile int64_t call_counter = 0;
+    int64_t seq = __atomic_fetch_add(&call_counter, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
     char filename[256];
-    snprintf(filename, 255, "/tmp/libfuzzer.%d", getpid());
+    snprintf(filename, sizeof(filename) - 1, "/dev/shm/bag-fuzz.%d.%lld",
+             (int)getpid(), (long long)seq);

     // Save input file to temporary file so that we can read it.
     FILE *fp = fopen(filename, "wb");
@@ -25,5 +42,6 @@
         pDataset->close();
     }

+    unlink(filename);
     return 0;
 }
